.\" hashpipe(1) -- man-macro reference page
.\" $Revision: 1.3 $
.\" $Log: hashpipe.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/08/29 17:35:32  dlr
.\" Two additions. Show the $HEX[] wrapper on output with a verified pair of equivalent invocations, plain colon-bearing password and hex-wrapped, both yielding the same line, and state that the password is everything after the preceding field so a colon parses. Separately, record that -X mode does NOT decode $HEX[] on -p, unlike the standalone hx command running the same language: a wrapped argument is hashed as the literal text. Verified -- hashpipe -X md5(pass) -p $HEX[616c706861] returns md5 of the literal string, while hx -p with the same argument returns md5 of alpha.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/08/29 17:16:00  dlr
.\" Remove the redundant .PP immediately after .SH EXAMPLES. mandoc reports it as "skipping paragraph macro: PP after SH"; groff does not flag it at all, which is how it survived a groff-only check. macOS and the BSDs render with mandoc, so mandoc is what most readers of these pages actually see. No rendered output changes.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: hashpipe(1) man page. Covers all 24 getopt options including the 11 absent from the built-in usage text (-L -G -X -F -p -S -P -u -D -Y -J). Documents the -L silent-decline hazard on expensive types, the -J exact-match output mode selector and HASHPIPE_OUTPUT, and the hx expression mode where -F is a script file rather than a hash file.
.\"
.TH HASHPIPE 1 "2026-08-29" "hashpipe 1.113" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
hashpipe \- verify and identify hash/password pairs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hashpipe
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.br
.B hashpipe
.B \-T
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hashpipe
.BI \-X " expression"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hashpipe
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hashpipe
answers one question per input line: does this password produce this hash,
and under which algorithm?  It is a verifier and identifier, not a searcher.
Where
.BR mdxfind (1)
runs a candidate list against a hash list,
.B hashpipe
takes candidate/hash pairs that are already paired up and confirms or
refutes them.
.PP
Input is read from the named
.IR file s,
or from standard input if none are given, as lines of the form
.PP
.RS
.RI [ TYPE [\fBx\fINN\fR]\  ] hash [\fB:\fIsalt\fR] \fB:\fIpassword
.RE
.PP
The leading type label is optional.  When it is absent
.B hashpipe
identifies the type itself by trying every registered algorithm whose
digest length matches, then \(em on the
.I hard pass
\(em by iterating the unsalted, non-composed types from depth 2 up to the
.B \-i
limit.
.PP
Verified lines are written to standard output in the same shape, with the
type and iteration depth filled in.  Lines that could not be resolved are
written unchanged to standard error.  The two streams are the result: a
clean split of solved from unsolved.
.PP
The password is everything after the field that precedes it, so a password
may itself contain a colon and still parse.  On output such a password is
wrapped in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
so the emitted line stays unambiguous, and the wrapped form is equally
accepted on input.  These two invocations are equivalent, and both produce
the same line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ echo '48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:pass:word' | hashpipe \-m e1
MD5x01 48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]

$ echo '48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]' \e
      | hashpipe \-m e1
MD5x01 48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B hashpipe
is CPU-only.  It has no GPU path and is not intended to acquire one.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Type selection and search depth"
.TP
.BI \-m " spec"
Restrict the search to the types in
.IR spec ,
a comma-separated list of bare
.BR hashcat (1)
mode numbers,
.BI e N
internal indices, and
.BI e N \- e M
ranges.  The token
.B auto
anywhere in the list re-enables full auto-detection as a fallback after the
listed types fail.  Restricting the type set is the single most effective
way to speed up a large run.
.TP
.BI \-i " count"
Maximum iteration depth for the hard pass.  Default 128.
.TP
.BI \-q " count"
Iteration step size for types that carry their own internal iteration
function.  Default 128.
.TP
.BI \-L " seconds"
Maximum estimated seconds for a single verify.  Default 1000.
.IP
A verify whose estimated cost exceeds this limit is
.I skipped,
and a skipped verify is reported exactly like a failed one: the line goes to
standard error as unresolved and the run reports nothing found.  There is no
distinguishing diagnostic.  Memory-hard and high-round types \(em scrypt,
Argon2, high-cost bcrypt, and anything with a parsed cost far above its
benchmark cost \(em routinely trip the default and need
.B \-L
raised explicitly.
.IP
The tell is wall-clock time.  If a verify of an expensive hash returns in
about 0.00 seconds, it did not run.  Time a single candidate before
believing any negative result on a slow type.
.BR mdxfind (1)
has no such guard, so its negative results on these types are real.
.TP
.BI \-t " count"
Worker thread count.  Defaults to the number of CPUs.
.SS "Output routing"
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Append verified results to
.IR file .
Default is standard output.
.TP
.BI \-O " file"
As
.BR \-o ,
but truncate
.I file
first.
.TP
.BI \-e " file"
Append unresolved lines to
.IR file .
Default is standard error.
.TP
.BI \-E " file"
As
.BR \-e ,
but truncate first.
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
Append run statistics to
.IR file .
In hx expression mode this same option supplies the salt instead.
.TP
.BI \-J " 0|1|2"
Output format.
.B 0
is mdxfind format,
.B 1
is John the Ripper format for input that arrived in John format, and
.B 2
is John format wherever a John equivalent exists.  The argument is matched
exactly; anything else is a fatal error rather than a silent fallback to
mode 0.  When a selected John mode has no label table compiled in,
.B hashpipe
says so on standard error and emits mdxfind format.
.SS "Self-test and benchmark"
.TP
.B \-T
Run the built-in self-tests over every registered type and report pass and
fail counts.  This is the first thing to run after a build.
.TP
.B \-G
As
.BR \-T ,
but also emit the generated example vectors.
.TP
.B \-B
Benchmark every registered type.
.TP
.BI \-b " spec"
Benchmark only the types in
.IR spec ,
using the same syntax as
.BR \-m .
.SS "hx expression mode"
These options turn
.B hashpipe
into a hash calculator driven by the
.BR hx (1)
expression language.  In this mode
.B hashpipe
computes and prints; it reads no hash file and performs no comparison.
.TP
.BI \-X " expression"
Evaluate
.I expression
against each password.
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Read the hx program from
.I file
instead of the command line.  Note that this is
.I not
a hash-file option; it does not correspond to
.BR "mdxfind \-F" .
.TP
.BI \-p " password"
Evaluate for this single password.  Without
.BR \-p ,
passwords are read one per line from standard input.
.IP
Unlike the rest of
.BR hashpipe ,
and unlike the standalone
.BR hx (1)
command, this mode does
.I not
decode
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
on the password.  A hex-wrapped argument is hashed as the literal text
.RB \(lq $HEX[...] \(rq,
brackets and all.  Pass the raw bytes instead, or use
.BR hx (1)
if the input has to be hex-wrapped.
.TP
.BI \-s " salt"
Bind the
.B salt
variable.
.TP
.BI \-S " salt2"
Bind the
.B salt2
variable.
.TP
.BI \-P " pepper"
Bind the
.B pepper
variable.
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
Bind the
.B user
variable.
.TP
.B \-D
Disassemble the compiled hx program and exit without running it.
.SS "Information"
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-Y
Load
.BR userdef.txt ,
print the load report, and exit.  Without
.B \-Y
the same file is loaded silently, so that pipe output is never polluted.
If
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
is unset, nothing is loaded and
.B \-Y
says so.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage summary followed by the full type table \(em internal
.BI e N
index, flags, name, and corresponding
.BR hashcat (1)
mode numbers.
.IP
Type flags are
.B s
salted,
.B u
uppercase-hex variant,
.B n
NTLM,
.B c
composed,
.B v
non-hex, and
.B V
has a dedicated verify function.
.SH "USER-DEFINED TYPES"
.B hashpipe
reads the same
.B userdef.txt
that
.BR mdxfind (1)
does, from the directory component of
.BR MDXFIND_CACHE ,
and identifies the types it declares.  With
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
unset, no user-defined types load at all and lines using them fall through
to unresolved with no diagnostic.
.PP
Bulk validation through user-defined types feeds an empty salt, so only
unsalted hx expressions can be validated this way.  The
.B \-X
and
.B \-F
options do not validate at all; they only compute.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HASHPIPE_OUTPUT
Selects the output format for callers that cannot pass
.BR \-J .
Same values.  An explicit
.B \-J
wins; when the environment variable is the one in effect,
.B hashpipe
announces it on standard error.
.TP
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
Path whose directory component holds
.BR userdef.txt .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage or argument error.
.B \-V
exits 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Confirm a build is sound:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-T
.fi
.RE
.PP
Split a mixed pot file into verified and unverified halves:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-O verified.txt \-E unresolved.txt potfile.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Identify unlabelled pairs, restricted to three plausible types, with
auto-detect as a fallback:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-m e1,e8,1000,auto < pairs.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Verify an expensive memory-hard type.  Without
.BR \-L ,
this reports nothing found in no time at all:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-m e1001 \-L 100000 < cmiyc.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compute a compound hash for one password:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-X 'sha1(md5(pass) . salt)' \-s deadbeef \-p password123
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR hx (1),
.BR mdsplit (1),
.BR getpass (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
